Independent Quantification of Electron and Ion Diffusion in Metallocene-doped Metal-Organic Frameworks Thin Films
Department of Chemistry, Virginia Tech, Blacksburg, Virginia, 24061, United States
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are prominent materials with considerable promise in many fields such as electrocatalysis, electronics and batteries.1, 2 These hybrid multidimensional arrays of inorganic nodes connected by organic ligands have arisen as potential candidates to satisfy the current demands for cleaner energy and its environmentally sustainable storage.3, 4 Recently, the fabrication of MOF thin films on conductive substrates, have facilitated their electrochemical characterization, and improved the utility of these systems in a wider variety of applications.3
Although originally most MOFs were considered electrical insulators, several examples of MOFs capable of efficient charge transport have been reported.5-7 Careful selection of ligands and nodes with appropriate electronic properties in the MOF synthesis, could lead to the extended structures containing charge transport pathways.6, 7 Similarly, post-synthetic modifications (PSM) of existing MOFs have shown to be an effective strategy for incorporation of electroactive moieties.8, 9 In particular, the use of coordinatively unsaturated metal clusters has been extensively employed to append the redoxactive species inside the frameworks.8, 10
While these synthetic approaches have contributed to the development of an extensive variety of conductive MOFs, detailed studies on the mechanism of electron transport in these materials are still lacking. In principle, the charge propagation in frameworks can be facilitated by three different mechanisms: 1) ligand-node orbital energy overlap,11 2) $\pi$ -stacking interactions between planar linkers,12 and 3) redox hopping between the neighboring centers.13 Indeed, the latter mechanism was demonstrated experimentally by quantifying the apparent diffusion coefficient $(D_{a p p})$ in Co-based MOF thin films, suggesting that charge conduction occurs through discrete ambipolar redox-hopping steps.13
If a redox hopping mechanism is operative, the electrochemical charge transport should depend on the rate of electron diffusion $(D_{e})$ from one redox center to another along the framework backbone, and the rate of ion diffusion $(D_{i})$ through the MOF pores to maintain the electroneutrality.13 The $D_{e}$ is related to the homogeneous self-exchange rate of the redox centers, whereas $D_{i}$ is primarily dependent on the ion size, MOF pore size, and the ion pairing ability. Although $D_{a p p}$ has been calculated by electrochemical and/or spectroscopic measurements, it lacks information regarding the contributions from electron diffusion and ion diffusion. Despite the importance of these diffusion terms for understanding the overall charge transport properties in MOFs, there are no detailed reports examining their relative contributions towards this process. This information is vital for establishing structurefunction relationships for the design of conductive frameworks.
Previously, a theoretical model capable of individually quantifying $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ was developed for idealized microcrystals immobilized on the surface of an electrode.14 In order to test the applicability of this model to the solid-state voltammetry of MOF thin films, we devised a series of metallocene-doped M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films on conductive fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) support. A series of chronoamperometry experiments in ${\mathrm{PF}}_{6}{}^{1}$ - and TFAB1- electrolytes was performed with the goal of unravelling the individual contributions of $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ in MOF thin films. The M-NU-1000 thin film system was chosen based on following considerations. (1) MOF NU-1000 is robust and chemically stable. (2) The nodes of NU-1000 have open sites that allow redox-active centers to be introduced by post-synthetic modification. (3) Metallocenes including ferrocene, ruthenocene, and osmocene (denoted as M; $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os), a class of well-studied redox couples that can undergo reversible one-electron oxidation reactions from $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ to $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ , 15, 16 can be incorporated as redox active centers into the framework. The use of such isostructural guests with varied self-exchange rates enables us to probe electron diffusion systematically. (4) Noncoordinating anions such as $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ and TFAB1-,17 combined with low donor solvents, such as dichloromethane (DCM), should facilitate unimpeded ion mobility during the electrochemical analysis.15 Moreover, we can gain insights into how ion diffusion is affected by counter anions with substantially different sizes. By applying the theoretical solid statevoltammetry model to the experimental data, we identified three key stages in the process of electrochemical transformation of M-NU-1000 films. To the best of our knowledge, this investigation is the first attempt to quantify $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ in the redox-hopping process in MOFs. Importantly, the $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{i}}$ values obtained by applying the theoretical model are generally within the same range of $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{app}}$ values $(10^{-14}-10^{-9}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-}$ 1) of MOFs from literature,13, 18, 19 suggesting good feasibility of applying this model to individually quantify $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ .
## 2. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The incorporation of the metallocene ligands in the structure of the NU-1000 to obtain M-NU-1000 ( $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) was achieved through a commonly employed solvent assisted ligand incorporation (SALI) procedure.10 NMR analysis of the digested M-NU-1000 samples (Figure S1) revealed that the ratio of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ to metallocene was 1:1 in all the frameworks, suggesting the presence of one metallocene molecule per zirconium node (Figure 1A). This type of NMR analysis has been successfully used in the literature to determine metallocene content in the NU-1000 MOF.10, 18 It is important to keep this ratio constant across the series, to ensure that metallocene concentration inside NU-1000 and the distance between them remains unchanged during the electrochemical characterization. Additionally, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of Fe-NU-1000, RuNU-1000, and Os-NU-1000 samples confirmed the expected NU-1000 structure, which remained consistent after the SALI procedure (Figure 1B). The deposition of these MOFs as thin films was achieved by electrophoretic deposition using conductive FTO slides as a support.10 SEM analysis showed an average film thickness of $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S2), and a hexagonal rod morphology of framework particles across the film surface, characteristic of NU-1000 microcrystals (Figure S3).
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) of the M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films was performed in a three-electrode assembly, using the M-NU-1000 film as the working electrode, a Pt mesh as a counter electrode, and either a ${\mathrm{TBAPF}}_{6}$ or TBATFAB dichloromethane solution $(0.1\mathbf{M})$ as the electrolyte (see SI for detailed electrochemical procedures). All the cyclic voltammograms of M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe},$ , Ru, Os) thin films showed a single reversible redox process, characteristic of the one-electron metallocene-centered redox transit between the $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ states $\mathbf{\mathrm{(M=Fe}}$ , Ru, Os) (Figure 2). The counter anion [A-] in the electrolyte solution $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ or TFAB1-) acts as a charge compensator to maintain electroneutrality, according to the following redox reaction:
In principle, the rate of the MOF redox reaction depends on both the rate of electron diffusion from one metallocene center to another (represented by the electron diffusion coefficient, $D_{e,}^{\setminus}$ ), and the rate of counter anion diffusion through the MOF pores to maintain the charge balance (represented by the ion diffusion coefficient, $D_{i}$ ). Specifically, $D_{e}$ should be dependent to the homogeneous self-exchange rate $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ to $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ ) of the metallocene centers ( $\mathrm{Fe}=k$ , $\mathrm{Ru}=2k$ , $\mathrm{Os}=4k\mathrm{\Omega}$ ).20, 21 $D_{i}$ on the other hand, should be directly related to the anion size $(\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1}$ -vol $=0.109\mathrm{nm}^{3}$ , $\mathrm{TFAB^{1-}v o l}_{\mathrm{vol}}^{-}{=}0.405\mathrm{nm}^{3})^{17}$ and the ion-pairing ability of the anion with respect to the metallocenium ion. CV analysis allowed the determination of the oxidation potentials for the $\mathrm{M}^{2+}/\mathrm{M}^{3+}$ redox couple for each of the six metallocene-counter anion combinations (Figure 2). These values $(E_{I/2})$ were used in the subsequent potential-step experiments.
To obtain the diffusion coefficients, chronoamperometry experiments were carried out on the Fe-NU-1000, Ru-NU1000, and Os-NU-1000 thin films (see SI for experimental details). A potential lower than the reduction peak $(E_{I})$ was applied for $20\mathrm{min}$ , followed by a potential $E_{2}$ that is higher than the oxidation peak for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Figure 3A). The current passed through the film at $E_{2}$ vs. time (I vs. t) plot was obtained (Figure 3B). It has been theoretically demonstrated that for solid state voltammetry of electrode-confined collection of microcrystals in contact with an electrolyte solution, this current-time response is governed by the electron and ion diffusion coefficients $\textstyle\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ ). A three-dimensional theoretical model to quantify the relative contribution of both $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ was developed by Scholz et al. who assumed that the electrochemical reaction starts at the electrolyte-crystalelectrode three-phase junction (Figure 3D).14 From this boundary, a simultaneous injection or removal of electrons and the corresponding dislocation of ions to maintain electroneutrality take place, as the reaction zone propagates into the crystal interior. Electron diffusion is postulated to be perpendicular to the electrode surface, while ion diffusion is parallel (Figure 3D). We envisioned that applying this model to the current-time response obtained for the Fe-NU-1000, RuNU-1000, and Os-NU-1000 thin films in two different electrolytes (Figure 4 A-F), could elucidate the values of $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ .
The model suggests the existence of three distinct reaction stages in the observed $I$ vs. t responses, representing the extent of redox conversion. When plotting the chronoamperometric response as a $I\sqrt{t}\ \mathrm{vs}.\sqrt{t}$ plot, these three stages (denoted as A, B, and C) become evident (Figure 3C). 14 During Stage A, both electron and ion diffusion are unhindered, and the faster process leads the spread of the initial charge transfer either along the crystal-solution interface or along the crystal-electrode boundary. Stage B begins with the maximum of the $I{\sqrt{t}}$ vs.√t curve (Figure 3C) that corresponds to the moment when all redox centers across such interface are oxidized. From this instant, the further course of the reaction into the crystal bulk is controlled by the slowest process. The very end of the conversion is the most characteristic segment (Stage C), where more than $99\%$ of the crystal is oxidized (Figure 3C). A schematic illustration of these three stages in the MOF is shown in Figure S7. The Scholz model provides current-time equations for each stage, as a function of several physical parameters, including the diffusion coefficients $(D_{e}$ and $D_{i})$ .14 These equations were used to fit the experimental data at each stage, to obtain the physical values of $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ and their evolution as the electrochemical reaction progresses.
First, the I√t vs.√t curves for the Fe-NU-1000, Ru-NU-1000, and Os-NU-1000 thin films in the two different electrolytes were constructed, as shown in Figure $4\mathrm{G}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{L}$ . For each of the six combinations of metallocene-counter anion couples, the chronoamperometry experiments were repeated four times on different films, in order to obtain the average $D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ values and the correspondent statistical error (see SI for detailed electrochemical procedures and a step-by-step sample calculation of the applied model at the three different stages). Once the three stages (A, B, and C) where identified from the $I{\sqrt{t}}$ vs.√t plots (Figure 4 G-L), the curve fitting of the experimental data to the appropriate current-time equation was carried out as follows:
Stage A (Figure 3C, and Figure 4 G-L). During Stage A, the model assumes that unhindered diffusion of both electrons and ions occurred through a quasi-semi-infinite space.14 The resulting current-time function for Stage A is expressed as:14
$$
\begin{array}{r}{I(t)=\frac{N F}{V_{m}}\Big\{\frac{1}{1+\exp{(-\varphi)}}\Big\}\Bigg[u\left(\left(\frac{\Delta x_{0}\sqrt{D_{e}}+\Delta z_{0}\sqrt{D_{i}}}{2\sqrt{\pi t}}\right)+\sqrt{D_{e}D_{i}}\right)-4D_{i}\sqrt{2D_{e}t}\Bigg]}\end{array}
$$
where $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ is the number of MOF crystallites in the film, $\mathrm{~F~}$ is Faraday’s constant, $u$ is the length of the three-phase junction (perimeter of the electrode-crystal interface), $\mathrm{V_{m}}$ is the MOF molar volume, $D_{e}$ is the electron diffusion coefficient, $D_{i}$ is the ion diffusion coefficient, $\Delta\mathbf{X}_{0}$ is the distance an ion travels in one hopping step, $\Delta z_{0}$ is the distance an electron travels in one hopping step, and
$$
\varphi=\frac{F}{R T}(E-E_{f})
$$
where $\mathtt{R}$ is the gas constant, $\mathrm{\DeltaT}$ is temperature, $E$ is the applied potential $E_{2}$ in Figure 3A), and $\mathrm{E_{f}}$ is the formal potential of metallocene $\mathrm{M}^{2+}/\mathrm{M}^{3+}$ couple. All of the above parameters can be empirically obtained from the crystal structure of NU-1000, SEM images of the films, and the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}.$ /metallocene ratio calculated from NMR digestion studies (See SI).
The fitting of Eq. 2 to the current- time response (I vs. t) from $\mathbf{t}=0$ s to $t_{r e f}$ (discussed in Stage B in detail) of all the M-NU1000 ( $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films in both electrolytes is shown in Figure 4 A-F (blue traces). A generally good fit $\mathrm{R}^{2}>96\%$ see SI) was observed for all the films, evidencing a close agreement between the experimental data and the theoretical model. The diffusion coefficients calculated in Stage A are presented in Figure 5 and summarized in Table 1.
Theoretically, two different cases can occur during Stage A, as the reaction zone spreads from the three-phase boundary: (1) If $D_{e}<D_{i}$ , this period is characterized by the rapid migration of ions along the electrode-crystal interface, where the redox active centers along this boundary are quickly converted.14 (2) If $D_{e}>D_{i},$ the reaction zone rapidly spreads away from the electrode in a perpendicular direction, along the electrolytecrystal interface (the crystal surface).
Application of the model to Stage A data reveals that the values of $D_{i}$ $(10^{-15}-10^{-18})$ across all the M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{\mathrm{M}}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films, regardless of the counter ion size, are significantly lower than those reported in literature for typical through-solution diffusion of ions $(10^{-5}-10^{-6})$ , Figure 5, Table 1.22 Prior to applying a potential, the MOF microcrystals are surrounded by a bath of counter ions $(0.1\mathrm{~M})$ , including ions located proximal to the crystal-electrolyte interface. As a result, ion diffusion is almost negligible during this initial stage and thus, the $D_{i}$ calculated are small. That said, the mechanism of electron/ion transport in Stage A is believed to follow the latter case $(D_{e}>D_{i})$ with rapid electron hopping along the crystal walls. The $D_{e}>D_{i}$ case is also supported in Stage B and C (vide infra).
Another important finding is the considerable variation in the $D_{e}$ values for the M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) series in Stage A (Figure 5- Stage A). The magnitude of electron diffusion followed the trend of the self-exchange rates of the metallocene centers. That is, $D_{e}$ increased in the order Fe-NU$\mathbf{1000<Ru-NU-1000<Os{\mathrm{-}}N U-1000}$ using $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ as the counter anion. This result highlights the interdependence of electron diffusion and the self-exchange rate of the redox centers, presenting the strategy to control the rate of electron transport in MOFs. A similar behavior was observed using TFAB1- as the counter anion, however the trend was weaker, the $D_{e}$ values varied in the order of $\mathbf{Fe}{\mathbf{-}}\mathbf{NU-1000}\approx\mathbf{Ru}{\mathbf{-}}\mathbf{NU-1000}<\mathbf{Os}{\mathbf{-}}\mathbf{NU-}$ 1000.
Stage B. The moment when all the redox centers at the electrolyte-crystal interface are oxidized, corresponds to the maximum of the $I\sqrt{t}\mathrm{vs}.\sqrt{t}$ curve (Figure 3C, and Figure 4 G-L). At this point, Eq. 2 loses its validity as diffusion is no longer assumed unhindered and ion diffusion into the crystallite becomes rate determining.14 Therefore, the theoretical model developed by Scholz et al.14 defined this maximum of the $N t$ vs.√t curves (Figure 3C), as a characteristic time $t_{r e f},$ that follows the mathematical expression:
$$
t_{r e f}=\frac{H^{2}}{1.1D_{e}}
$$
where $\mathrm{~H~}$ is the thickness of the MOF particles $(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m},$ , according to SEM images, Figure S2). As a result, the current detected at this time $(I_{r e f})$ can be expressed as:14
$$
I_{r e f}=0.72\frac{N F u}{V_{m}}\sqrt{D_{e}D_{i}}
$$
$D_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ in Stage B were calculated using Eq.4 and Eq.5 and are summarized in Figure 5 and Table 1. Since the process is limited by ion movement, the values of $D_{e}\left(\approx10^{-9}-10^{-10}\right)$ were again found to be higher than those of $D_{i}(\approx10^{-11}-10^{-14})$ for all the metallocenes in both electrolytes. Importantly, the $D_{e}$ values in Stage B are lower than those obtained in Stage A. As mentioned above, electrons predominantly hop along the crystal-electrolyte boundary that is perpendicular to the substrate in Stage A. In contrast, electrons in Stage B (beginning from $t_{r e f}$ ) are gradually diffusing deep into the MOF crystals. Metallocenes inside MOF pores tend to have lower degrees of freedom compared to those anchored on the outer surface of MOF crystals. As a result, redox centers at the crystalelectrolyte interface can orient more efficiently, allowing for faster electron diffusion in Stage A. Similarly, the overall ion diffusion $(D_{i})$ has increased across the series in Stage B compared to the negligible values obtained in Stage A. This fact illustrates that only ions that were initially at the electrolytecrystal interface were satisfying the electroneutrality requirement in Stage A, while from $t_{r e f},$ ion diffusion occurred from the crystal-electrolyte interface to the bulk of the particle through the MOF channels (Stage B).
One of the key conclusions of this modelling was that the principal trends in diffusion coefficients observed for the MNU-1000 $\mathbf{\mathrm{M}}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films in Stage A remained largely unchanged in Stage B (Figure 5). $D_{e}$ increased generally following the order $\mathbf{Fe-NU-1000}<\mathbf{Ru-NU-1000}<\mathbf{Os-NU-}$ 1000 when using either $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ or TFAB- as the counter anion. This result reconfirms the dependence of electron diffusion on the self-exchange rate of the redox centers. Essentially, the similarities in the diffusion coefficient trends $\textstyle\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ ) for the M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films at both Stage A and Stage B, suggest that the redox hopping rate can be straightforwardly modulated (and potentially maximized) through the incorporation of molecular moieties with rapid selfexchange rates into a MOF structure containing large channels, as well as employing electrolytes based on smaller counter ions.
Stage C. After the rapid exhaustion of the metallocene centers at the electrolyte-crystal interface (Stage A), the further course of the reaction into the crystal bulk (Stage B, starting from maximum of the I√t vs.√t curve) is controlled by the ability of ions to propagate into the MOF interior and provide the charge balance. As the majority of the MOF is oxidized (Figure 3C and Figure 4 G-L), ion migration through the framework channels becomes extremely important, and the last period of the electrochemical conversion is determined by the dimensions of the MOF crystallites. As a consequence, the chronoamperometric curves for Stage C can be modeled exclusively in terms of the ion diffusion coefficients $(D_{i})$ , and the crystal dimensions:14
$$
I(t)=\frac{N F L H D_{i}}{V_{m}B}\left\{\frac{1}{1+\exp\left(-\varphi\right)}\right\}e x p\left[\frac{-\pi D_{i}t}{B^{2}}\right]
$$
where $\mathrm{~L~}$ and B are the average length and the average width of the MOF particles, respectively (according to SEM images, Figure S3). It is important to note that Eq. 6 has been developed for the very last segment of Stage C, where the total conversion of the crystal is higher than $99\%$ (Figure 3C).14
Table 1. Summary of the electron $(D_{e})$ and ion $(D_{i})$ diffusion coefficients for M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films in $\operatorname{PF}_{6}{}^{1.}$ and TFAB1-based electrolytes
The fitting of Eq. 6 to the current-time response (I vs. t) from the moment where $99\%$ charge has passed to the end of all the M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films in both electrolytes is shown in Figures 4 A-F (red traces) (see SI for detail). A generally good fit $R^{2}>90\%$ , see SI) was achieved, evidencing a good agreement between the experimental data and the theoretical model. The diffusion coefficients obtained in Stage C are presented in Figure 5 and summarized in Table 1. As calculated, the $D_{i}$ values in this final stage are higher than those obtained in both Stage A and Stage B, for all the M-NU-1000 $\mathbf{\mathrm{T}}\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films. In fact, the last segment of Stage C represents the moment of $99\%$ of conversion. Thus, instead of transport from solution to crystal interior, ion diffusion in this last stage is dominated by short-distance metallocene-tometallocene hopping since the electroneutrality requirement is almost totally satisfied across the particle. Therefore, the values obtained for $D_{i}$ describe an instant where the diffusion conditions are similar to a typical twin-electrode thin film cell.14
Interestingly, ion diffusion of TFAB1- was generally faster compared to $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ as the counter anion for all the M-NU-1000 1 $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) thin films (Figure 5 - Stage C). Despite the large ionic size of the TFAB1- anion,17 its ability to associate with the $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os) cations is weak. In contrast, the $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ anion associates strongly with the $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ species.15 As a result, the highly associated $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ anion displays a lower degree of mobility inside the MOF pores, due to strong ion pairing.23 Conversely, the lower degree of ion-pair association displayed by the TFAB1- anion, results in easier hopping of TFAB1- between metallocene units.23 Certainly, both the ionic size and the ability to associate with the $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ cation are factors that significantly contribute to the reorganization energy of the system, and therefore affect the rate of electron transfer.24 Thus, a higher degree of ion-pair association, and a smaller ionic size not only will lead to less ion mobility through the MOF pores, but will also reduce the reorganization energy of the system facilitating the electron transport.25
## 3. CONCLUSION
In summary, the systematic variation of metallocene redox center and counter anions in a series of chronoamperometry experiments allowed the elucidation of the relative contribution of electron and ion diffusion in metallocene-doped NU-MOF thin films. The electron diffusion coefficient $D_{e}$ in both Stages A and B was found to increase in the order of Fe-NU- $\mathbf{1000}<$ $\mathbf{Ru{-}N U{-}1000<O s{-}N U{-}1000}$ , in parallel to the self-exchange rates of the $\mathbf{M}^{2+}/\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ couples $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Ru, Os). Ion diffusion $(D_{i})$ of $\mathrm{TFAB^{1-}}$ was generally faster compared to $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1.}$ , highlighting the easier ion mobility due to lower ion-pairing association. During all stages, ion diffusion is the limiting process in redox hopping. The trends observed in the diffusion coefficients $\textstyle\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_{e}$ and $D_{i}$ ) of these systems obtained in $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{1-}$ and TFAB1- based electrolytes at the different stages of the electrochemical reaction, demonstrated that the electron redox hopping rates inside frameworks can be controlled through the modifications of the self-exchange rates of redox centers and proper selection of counter anions. The successful application of a solid state-voltammetry theoretical model to the experimental chronoamperometry data of MOF thin films, is a significant advancement to the understanding of electron and ion mobility in the context of electrochemical experiments, and can be used to guide further research into other redox-active MOFs.
